<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Communist Manifesto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Communist Manifesto, Manifesto of the Communist Party</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Communist Manifesto, originally the Manifesto of the Communist Party, is a political pamphlet by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a>, commissioned by the Communist League and published anonymously in German in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in February 1848. It argues that &quot;the history of all hitherto existing society is the history of class struggles&quot; and predicts that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> will overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a> and bring about a classless society. It fell into obscurity after the defeat of the revolutions of 1848, revived in the 1870s and has become one of the world's most influential political documents.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto.jpg" alt="The Communist Manifesto" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Friedrich Engels, Karl Marx · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Communist-manifesto.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels, whose <b>Communist Manifesto</b> appeared in 1848, argued that the working class must take power and end the private ownership of the means of production.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Industrial Revolution advanced, socialist critics blamed capitalism for the misery of the proletariat of factory workers, and in 1848 Marx and Engels offered a new definition of communism in <b>The Communist Manifesto</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Marx and Engels wrote <b>The Communist Manifesto</b> in 1847–48, just before the Revolutions of 1848, expressing what they termed scientific socialism, and by the late 19th century socialism had come to mean anti-capitalism and a post-capitalist system based on some form of social ownership.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Lennon called it &quot;anti-religious, anti-nationalistic, anti-conventional, anti-capitalistic&quot;, but accepted because it was &quot;sugarcoated&quot;, and said it was close to <b>The Communist Manifesto</b> although he was not a Communist; Ono described it as what Lennon believed, that all are one country, one world, one people.</blockquote><a href="/songs/imagine">Imagine</a></li></ul></section>
